# ─── Redash backend ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REDASH_URL: server-side only, the /api/redash/* proxy and /api/auth/* routes
# forward requests here. Never exposed to the browser.
# Port 5001, not 5000: ../node/compose.yaml publishes the server as
# "5001:5000". On macOS port 5000 is also held by ControlCenter (AirPlay).
REDASH_URL=http://localhost:5001

# REDASH_INTERNAL_API_KEY: server-side only. Used EXCLUSIVELY by the admin
# proxy routes (/api/admin/*), and only after verifying the caller's own
# session carries the permission Redash puts on the endpoint being proxied
# (INTERNAL_KEY_ENDPOINTS in src/lib/redash-server.ts; today that is
# super_admin for all three). Regular data requests authenticate with the
# user's own Redash session cookie so Redash enforces per-user permissions.
#
# This is a Redash USER api_key, and Redash runs the request as that key's
# owner with that owner's permissions, so the owner must be a super admin for
# those endpoints to answer at all. That is exactly why the gate in front of it
# cannot be weaker: the key is never the caller.
REDASH_INTERNAL_API_KEY=

# NEXT_PUBLIC_REDASH_URL: toggles the client between mock mode (unset) and
# real-backend mode (set). The value itself is only used as a truthy flag, # all traffic still goes through the same-origin /api/redash/* proxy.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_REDASH_URL=http://localhost:5001

# ─── Instance config (white-label) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Path to the YAML instance config. Default: veodyn.config.yaml in the app
# root. See veodyn.config.example.yaml. A missing file yields neutral defaults.
# VEODYN_CONFIG_PATH=./veodyn.config.yaml
#
# Any config key can be overridden by env with __ as the nesting separator:
# VEODYN_BRAND__NAME=Meridian

# ─── AI (creation) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AI creation is config-driven. Set ai.enabled and ai.endpoint together in
# veodyn.config.yaml, or use these equivalent instance config overrides.
# When disabled, AI endpoints return 403 and all AI affordances stay hidden.
# VEODYN_AI__ENABLED=true
# The endpoint is a provider implementing the five routes the relay calls
# (generate-sql, outline, report, digest, suggest-annotations). veodyn-api
# serves them under /ai, so pointing at a local one looks like this:
# VEODYN_AI__ENDPOINT=http://localhost:8010/ai
# The provider key is server-only, env only, and never belongs in the YAML.
# It is sent as `Authorization: Bearer` and must match the provider's own
# VEODYN_AI_RELAY_KEY. The browser's cookie is deliberately NOT forwarded, so
# this is the only credential the provider sees.
# VEODYN_AI__KEY=

# ─── Optional surfaces ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Features an instance can switch on, off unless it does. Query Snippets is
# reusable SQL fragments expanded by a trigger word: with none written it is an
# empty page holding a permanent nav row, so it ships off. Off means the route
# answers 404 and the nav entry is absent, not a greyed-out button.
# VEODYN_FEATURES__QUERY_SNIPPETS=true

# ─── veodyn-api: one service, three env names ───────────────────────────────
# CATALOG_API_URL, KPI_API_URL and REPORTS_API_URL below are three aliases for
# the SAME veodyn-api root: it serves /catalog, /tags, /favorites and, where the
# enterprise pack is installed, /kpis and /reports, all off one host. They are
# separate vars because each contract was wired up on its own schedule, and
# deployments set different subsets of them.
# /api/tags therefore takes whichever of the three is set (KPI first), and only
# 503s when none is. Set at least one of them to get tag editing.

# ─── Catalog backend (Data Catalog contract) ────────────────────────────────
# Server-side only. The /api/catalog and /api/domains/[key] handlers proxy here.
# Unset => those endpoints return 503 and the app runs on mock fixtures.
# veodyn-api serves /catalog off the historical ClickHouse warehouse, so this is
# the veodyn-api root (see api/veodyn_api/routers/catalog.py).
# CATALOG_API_URL=http://localhost:8010

# ─── KPI and Reports backends ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Server-side only, and both still read by this build even though the KPI and
# report SURFACES are not part of it: /api/favorites takes the first of
# KPI_API_URL and REPORTS_API_URL that is set, and /api/tags takes the first of
# all three aliases. Unset => those two endpoints 503 and the app runs on mock
# fixtures.
#
# They are declared here, and in src/lib/env.ts, rather than left to the
# enterprise overlay to add. The env schema is closed (an undeclared read throws
# at import time and the whole app fails to boot, not just that endpoint), so a
# variable an overlay needed but the public schema did not declare would force
# the overlay to PATCH src/lib/env.ts. The sibling tenant pack does exactly that
# and its patch broke once when an unrelated public refactor moved the anchor it
# inserted against. Two community endpoints want these values anyway, so there
# is nothing speculative about keeping them: the enterprise /kpis and /reports
# proxy routes read the same two names and need no schema change at all.
#
# veodyn-api serves every one of these contracts on 8090 in the local compose
# stack (8080 is commonly taken).
# KPI_API_URL=http://localhost:8090
# REPORTS_API_URL=http://localhost:8090

# ─── Demo fixture pack (mock mode) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Which mock dataset seeds the app when no real backend is configured.
# Unset or "neutral" (default) = generic demo. "la" selects the tenant pack,
# which is not in this repository (see app/next.config.ts) and resolves back to
# the neutral pack unless an overlay build supplied it.
# Build-time flag (Next inlines NEXT_PUBLIC_*).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DEMO_PACK=neutral

# ─── Visualization plugins ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Which plugin packages this image installs, comma-separated. Unset (the
# default) registers the core visualization types only, which is the stock OSS
# product. Known package names live in src/plugins/index.ts; a name that is not
# one of them warns in the console and is ignored. Build-time flag, because the
# bundler has to see the import. Admin > Plugins reports what actually
# registered.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_VEODYN_PLUGINS=example

# ─── Telemetry (PostHog, optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Either one empty disables telemetry entirely, browser and server. The key is
# a client-side ingestion token that posthog-js ships to every browser, not a
# secret. Set on stage only; the prod release deliberately leaves these unset,
# which is how one image serves both.
POSTHOG_KEY=
POSTHOG_HOST=
# Kill switch that wins over a configured key.
DISABLE_TELEMETRY=
# Forward console.warn/error as client_console events. Off by default, because
# it widens the event surface considerably.
TELEMETRY_CONSOLE_FORWARDING=
# Stamped by the build so an event can be pinned to a release and a commit.
APP_VERSION=
APP_COMMIT=
